course: everyday
lesson: 246
title: Music practice and ball games
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Learning an instrument is like building a wall brick by brick: a little practice every day works better than one long session a week.
explain: When a piece has a hard bar (a short part of the music), you play it slowly until it is right, because playing a mistake fast many times turns it into a habit.
explain: Instruments are delicate and often expensive: you carry them in their case, keep them out of hot cars and ask before touching one in a shop.
explain: In ball games, safety and fairness come first: protect your face from a flying ball, throw low in dodgeball, and let everybody have a turn.
explain: A boy in Perth practises his recorder for fifteen minutes every evening, and after a month he plays better than his friend who practised for two hours on Sundays.
fact: Short daily practice builds musical skill faster than one long practice a week.
fact: A hard bar is practised slowly until it is right, because fast mistakes become a habit.
fact: Mouthpieces of trumpets and recorders are cleaned and not shared without cleaning, because germs pass between mouths.
fact: Instruments are carried in their cases and never left in a hot car, where they can crack.
fact: Drums are practised at agreed times with a practice pad or quiet sticks, so family and neighbours are not disturbed.
fact: When someone performs, you listen quietly and clap at the end, because it shows respect for their effort.
fact: In a choir or band you come on time and follow the conductor, because group music only works together.
fact: You ask before touching instruments in a music shop, because they are expensive and belong to the shop.
fact: Piano keys are pressed gently, because banging them can damage the keys and the hammers inside.
fact: When a ball flies at your face, you put your hands up and turn your head.
fact: In dodgeball you throw below the shoulders and never aim at heads.
fact: You never kick or throw a ball at someone who is not ready, because it hurts and starts fights.
fact: A ball stuck on a roof is a job for an adult; you never climb onto a roof.
fact: You keep a safe distance from someone swinging a racket.
exercise: How should you practise a new instrument? | A little every day | A lot, but only once a week | Only before a concert | Only when you feel like it
exercise: One bar of your piano piece keeps going wrong. What do you do? | Play it slowly until it is right | Play the whole piece through again, but faster | Skip that bar every time you get to it | Play it louder
exercise: Why should you not play your mistakes fast again and again? | The mistakes become a habit | The piano slowly goes out of tune | Your fingers grow tired forever | The teacher cannot hear them
exercise: Your friend wants to try your recorder. What should happen first? | Clean the mouthpiece | Nothing, it is fine | Blow into it harder | Put it in your pocket
exercise: You carry the school violin home. How should you carry it? | In its case | Under your arm without the case | In a plastic bag | By its strings
exercise: Why should a violin not stay in a hot car? | Heat can crack it | Heat makes it play louder | The strings grow longer | Heat makes it smell bad
exercise: You play drums at home. What is considerate? | Practise at agreed times with a practice pad | Play as loudly as you like late at night | Practise whenever you happen to wake up, even at dawn | Play with the windows open
exercise: Your cousin plays a song on the guitar for you. What do you do? | Listen quietly and clap at the end | Talk with your friend while she plays | Play on your phone | Leave the room halfway through
exercise: In the school band, who do you follow? | The conductor | The loudest player | Whoever sits next to you | Your own speed
exercise: Your little brother bangs the piano keys with his fists. What can you do? | Show him how to press the keys gently | Close the lid down on his hands so he learns | Shout at him to go away | Let him bang it even harder to get it out of his system
exercise: In a music shop you see a shiny guitar. What should you do? | Ask before touching it | Pick it up and play it | Tap it to hear the sound | Turn the tuning pegs
exercise: A ball is flying straight at your face. What do you do? | Put your hands up and turn your head | Close your eyes tight and stand very still | Open your mouth wide to shout a warning | Lean forward to get a better look at it
exercise: Where do you aim when you throw in dodgeball? | Below the shoulders | At the head | Right at the face, as hard as you can | At the back of the neck
exercise: Your ball lands on the garage roof. What do you do? | Ask an adult to get it | Climb up the drainpipe | Climb on the bins to reach it | Stand on the car roof
exercise: Four square at recess has a line of waiting children. What is fair? | Learn the rules and let others have a turn | Stay in the square for the whole of break time | Only let your own friends play | Make up new rules as you go so that you always win
exercise: Your friend is swinging a tennis racket. Where do you stand? | A safe distance away | Right behind him | Close in front to watch | Next to his swinging arm
test: Maya practises the flute for 3 hours every Saturday, and Leo practises for 20 minutes every day. Who will probably improve faster? | Leo, because daily practice builds skill faster | Maya, because she practises longer at once | Both exactly the same | Neither, because practice does not help
test: A girl in Ohio leaves her school cello in the car on a hot summer day. What could happen? | The wood could crack | It would sound better | Nothing at all | The strings would tighten into tune
test: Why is it polite to sit quietly while your sister plays at a concert? | It shows respect for her effort | It makes her play faster | The rules of concerts forbid all clapping | So that she cannot hear you
test: Two children share one trumpet in a lesson. What keeps them from passing germs? | Cleaning the mouthpiece between players | Blowing much harder into it | Playing only quiet notes | Holding the trumpet with gloves on the whole time
test: In dodgeball a boy in Glasgow throws hard at a girl's head. Why is this wrong? | Head hits can injure | It is too easy to catch | The ball may burst | It wastes the ball
test: Tom kicks the ball at Ben's back while Ben is tying his shoelace. Why is that wrong? | Ben was not ready, it hurts and it starts fights | Ben should have been watching the ball all the time | It is fine if it is only a joke | The ball might get dirty
test: A frisbee lands on the roof of the school shed. What is the safe choice? | Tell a teacher and let an adult fetch it | Climb up onto the roof using the fence | Throw stones at it until it falls down | Get a friend to give you a boost up
test: Why does a choir sound bad if each singer goes at their own speed? | Group music only works when everyone follows together | Choirs need everyone to sing loudly | The conductor stops listening | The songs become too short
test: A toddler in Auckland hammers a piano with toy blocks. Why should you stop him gently? | The keys and hammers inside can be damaged | The piano will start to play all by itself | The blocks will become stuck forever | The neighbours will call the police about the noise
test: piano exam in 2 weeks, gonna practise 3 hours on sunday. enough? | Short practice every day works better | Yes, one long day is best | Practise only the day before | No practice, just play on the day
test: friend wants a go on my recorder, just hand it over? | Clean the mouthpiece first, germs pass between mouths | Yes, recorders don't carry germs | Yes, if they blow softly | Only lick it clean first
test: our ball's stuck on the garage roof, i can climb up! | No, ask an adult to get it | Yes, garage roofs are low | Yes, if you're quick | Stand on your bike to reach it
test: dodgeball rn, where do i aim | Below the shoulders, never at heads | At the face, it's easier to hit | At whoever isn't looking | At the smallest kid, they can't dodge
explain: A boy in New York practised the drums for hours every day without ear protection; after a few months he had ringing in his ears that would not go away.
explain: A girl in Australia batted in cricket practice without a helmet; the ball hit her face, broke her nose and chipped two teeth.
fact: Very loud sound, played often, damages hearing for good.
fact: A hard ball moves fast enough to break a nose, a tooth or a cheekbone, which is why helmets, face guards and mouth guards are worn.
fact: For a nosebleed, sit down, lean forward, pinch the soft part of the nose and tell an adult; do not tip your head back.
test: drum practice for 3 hours, no earplugs needed right | Wear ear protection, loud sound damages hearing | Right, music can't hurt your ears | Right, only shouting hurts ears | Only for the first hour, after that ur ears get used to it
test: Why does the coach make Ava wear a helmet to bat? | A fast ball can break her face or skull | It keeps the sun off | It helps her see the ball | Helmets make the bat swing faster
test: ball hit my nose, it's bleeding | Sit, lean forward, pinch the soft part and tell an adult | Tip your head back | Lie flat on the ground | Blow your nose hard
test: did u hear a kid climbed on the school roof to get the ball and fell off? real lesson? | A ball on a roof is a job for an adult | Climb up quickly before a teacher sees | Roofs are safe if you're careful | Throw another ball to knock it down
test: apparently someone got hit in the face in dodgeball and his nose broke in 3 places. what's the true lesson? | Throw below the shoulders and never at heads | Dodgeball is only safe with soft balls at heads | Aim at the head to win faster | Faces don't break from balls, he must have fallen too
test: my mate says if u practise piano for 7 hours on Sunday it's the same as every day. right? | No, short daily practice builds skill faster | Yes, the total is what counts | Yes, longer is always better | No, practice doesn't help at all
exam: Identify the more effective pattern of musical practice. | Short practice every day | A single long practice each week | Practice only before a concert | Practice only when one feels like it
exam: Which of the following constitutes the appropriate response when a ball flies towards one's face? | Raising the hands and turning the head | Standing perfectly still with the eyes closed | Leaning forward towards the ball | Opening the mouth wide in order to shout
exam: Select the response consistent with established practice concerning the mouthpieces of instruments. | They are cleaned and not shared without cleaning | They may be shared freely between friends | They require no cleaning at all | They are cleaned only once a year, at the end of the summer term
exam: Ascertain the reason for which instruments are not left in a hot car. | They may crack in the heat | They become louder when warm | They may be stolen in the heat | They lose their colour in sunlight
